  <p>
    One wrong dropdown on a Google Business Profile kept Motha Electric — a company that had been
    doing electrical work for 46 years — invisible to everyone searching “electrician.” Their
    primary category said <strong>electrical installation service</strong>. Changing it to
    <strong>electrician</strong> took about thirty seconds, cost nothing, and produced one to three
    calls a day within a week.
  </p>
  <p>
    They were paying an SEO company the whole time. Nobody had looked.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens when a proven ad setup produces nothing</h2>
  <p>
    Early on at Local Service Rocket I took on Motha Electric. They were already on retainer with
    an SEO company and weren’t getting leads, which is a common enough starting point that I didn’t
    think much of it. So I did the obvious thing: I built out
    <a href="/services/paid-ads">Google Ads</a> using the exact structure that was working for
    another electrical client at the time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing. Not slow, not expensive — nothing worth having.
  </p>
  <p>
    That’s the useful part of the story, because when the same system prints money in one account
    and dies in another, you can stop blaming the algorithm. Something underneath the ads is
    different. So I stopped spending and went looking at the foundation.
  </p>

  <h2>What was actually wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Their Google Business Profile listed the primary category as “electrical installation service.”
    It’s a real Google category. It’s even arguably accurate — they do install electrical systems.
    It is also almost entirely useless.
  </p>
  <p>
    I pulled up Google Keyword Planner and checked what people in their market actually type. The
    dominant term, by an enormous margin, was <strong>electrician</strong>. Searches for
    “electrical installation service” were a rounding error. Nobody with a dead outlet opens their
    phone and types “electrical installation service near me.”
  </p>
  <p>
    Your primary category is the single clearest statement you make to Google about what your
    business is. Google’s job is to hand a searcher a business it is confident is relevant. If your
    profile insists you’re a niche installation contractor, Google will hesitate to show you for
    “electrician near me” — and your ads inherit that same confusion as weak relevance and a worse
    cost per click. The ads weren’t broken. The identity underneath them was.
  </p>

  <h2>The only change we made</h2>
  <p>
    We took over the profile and switched the primary category to Electrician.
  </p>
  <div class="receipt">
    <div class="receipt-label">WHAT HAPPENED NEXT</div>
    <p>
      Within about a week, one to three calls a day were coming in from the Google Business Profile
      alone. The ads weren’t even the hero of that turnaround — the free listing was.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    No new website. No content strategy. No link building. No increase in spend. A dropdown.
  </p>

  <h2>Why does this keep happening to good companies?</h2>
  <p>
    Motha had been in business 46 years. They weren’t naive, and they weren’t cheap — they were
    paying a marketing company every month. The problem is that agencies are usually organised as a
    set of specialists: someone on content, someone on links, someone on ads. Each one does their
    task competently.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    The problem wasn’t hard. It was nobody’s job.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Checking whether Google understands what the business is doesn’t belong to the content person
    or the ads person. It belongs to whoever is accountable for the whole account, and at a lot of
    agencies that person doesn’t exist. This is exactly why we
    <a href="/get-started">run a surgeon model instead</a>: one expert who owns the outcome and is
    expected to look at everything, including the boring parts that don’t generate a monthly
    deliverable.
  </p>

  <h2>Relevance comes before authority</h2>
  <p>
    SEO conversation defaults to blogs, backlinks and “content strategies.” Those matter — later.
    When Google isn’t sure what you are, none of it lands, because you’re trying to build authority
    for a thing you haven’t established yet.
  </p>
  <p>For a new or misunderstood brand, the order is:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Relevance.</strong> Teach Google exactly what you do, in the words people search:
      correct primary category, honest service areas, page titles and H1s that use the trade name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Authority and trust.</strong> Reviews — especially recent ones — proof of work,
      consistent name and address everywhere, real service pages. See
      <a href="/services/local-seo">how we run local SEO</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Expansion.</strong> More service-area pages, more topics, more sophisticated content,
      once the core is genuinely solid.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Big brands get to skip step one. Apple doesn’t need the word “phone” in a headline, because the
    entire internet already tells Google what Apple is. Your plumbing company is not Apple. Until
    Google is completely certain what you do, be radically, almost boringly clear.
  </p>

  <h2>What to check this week</h2>
  <p>
    This is a fifteen-minute audit you can do yourself, without hiring anyone:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Open Keyword Planner and find the one term homeowners actually type for your trade in your
      city. There’s almost always a clear winner.
    </li>
    <li>Does your GBP primary category use that word? If not, change it today.</li>
    <li>
      Search that term and look at who ranks. Google shows each competitor’s primary category next
      to their name — if the top three all say something you don’t, that’s your answer.
    </li>
    <li>Do your homepage title tag and H1 say the same thing, without stuffing it into every line?</li>
    <li>
      Are you winning on <em>review velocity</em> — new reviews per week — rather than a big total
      you earned three years ago?
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Fix the boring foundation first. That’s where most of the money is hiding, and it’s the part
    most vendors never look hard enough at, because there’s no invoice line item for a dropdown.
  </p>
